---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import MissFigure from '../../components/application-caching/misses-pilot/MissFigure.astro';
const { hrefFor } = Astro.props;
const wrapper = `// Group shares one source load per key among the callers waiting for it.
type Group struct {
	sf    singleflight.Group
	Load  func(ctx context.Context, key string) (Card, error) // recheck cache, read source, fill
	Stale func(key string) (Card, bool)                      // a copy freshness still permits
}

func (g *Group) Get(ctx context.Context, key string) (Card, error) {
	ch := g.sf.DoChan(key, func() (any, error) {
		// Detach from the first caller, then give the shared load its own budget.
		lctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.WithoutCancel(ctx), 120*time.Millisecond)
		defer cancel()
		return g.Load(lctx, key)
	})
	select {
	case r := <-ch:
		if r.Err != nil {
			return "", r.Err
		}
		return r.Val.(Card), nil
	case <-ctx.Done(): // this caller's deadline: leave without starting another read
		if c, ok := g.Stale(key); ok {
			return c, nil
		}
		return "", ctx.Err()
	}
}`;
const stampedeActivity = `select state, wait_event, count(*), max(now() - query_start) as oldest, left(query, 40) as query
from pg_stat_activity where backend_type = 'client backend' group by 1, 2, 5 order by 3 desc;

 state  | wait_event | count |     oldest      |                  query
--------+------------+-------+-----------------+------------------------------------------
 active | PgSleep    |   222 | 00:00:00.556104 | select name || ':' || price_pence /*card`;
const leaseTrace = `   0 ms  A  SET lease:list:… tokA NX PX 200      -> OK
  11 ms  B  SET lease:list:… tokB NX PX 200      -> (nil)   serves the older list
 212 ms  B  SET lease:list:… tokB NX PX 200      -> OK
 265 ms     pg_stat_activity: 2 active list computations
 364 ms  B  owner-checked publish                -> 1
 364 ms  B  owner-checked release                -> 1
 902 ms  A  source query finished
 902 ms  A  owner-checked publish                -> 0
 902 ms  A  owner-checked release                -> 0      cache keeps B's list`;
const ownerChecks = `-- Publish: KEYS[1] lease, KEYS[2] list; ARGV[1] token, ARGV[2] list, ARGV[3] TTL in ms
if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
  redis.call('SET', KEYS[2], ARGV[2], 'PX', ARGV[3])
  return 1
end
return 0

-- Release: KEYS[1] lease; ARGV[1] token
if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
  return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1])
end
return 0`;
---
<p>A cache miss sends a request back to the work the cache was meant to save. At the shop’s peak, 20,000 product-card requests a second reach 20 <code>Server</code> instances, and about 94% of them find a usable card in Redis. The rest, about 1,200 a second, read PostgreSQL, assemble the card and store it again. In a load test the database sustained about 2,500 card fills a second before checkout began missing its latency objective, so the card cache is allowed 2,000.</p>
<p>On an average second that margin is comfortable. Misses do damage when they arrive together. A popular entry expires and every request that arrives before its replacement is stored starts its own source read. Redis restarts empty, and all 20,000 requests a second miss at once. Redis becomes unreachable, and every <code>Server</code> turns to the database instead. Each time, the source receives a burst that the average hit ratio never shows, and a source that slows under the burst lengthens it.</p>
<p>Handling misses means deciding what each caller does when its answer is missing. It can start source work or wait for someone else’s. It can take an older answer that is still permitted, or a reduced one. Or it can be refused quickly.</p>
<p>Five mechanisms make those decisions. Request coalescing shares one fill among the callers of a key. Deadlines bound how long callers wait and how long fills run. Early refresh replaces hot entries before they expire. Admission control caps source work across every key and every <code>Server</code>. And the same cap holds when the cache fails or comes back empty. Each one solves a problem the others leave open.</p>
<p>Two cached answers carry the examples. Product P7’s card, the blue mug, is requested about 250 times a second and takes 20 ms to fill. North’s list of products under £21 is linked from the home page. Its first page is requested about 400 times a second and takes about 150 ms of database time to compute.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="one-expiry">
<h2 id="one-expiry">One expiry can start many reads</h2>
<p>The under-£21 list has just expired. The first request misses and starts computing it. The next request arrives 2.5 ms later, finds the entry still missing, and starts a second computation, because nothing in the cache records that the first has begun. Every request in the next 150 ms does the same, until the first result is stored. Many callers rebuilding the same missing answer at once is a <strong>cache stampede</strong>, also called a thundering herd or a dog-pile.</p>
<p>Its size is the arrival rate times the time to the first fill: 400 a second for 0.15 s is 60 computations of the same list. Compare that with what the source is allowed. At 2,000 fills a second and 20 ms each, the whole fleet may run 40 card fills at any moment. Normal misses already keep about 24 of them busy. One expiry of one list asks for 60 more, each seven times as long as a card fill.</p>
<MissFigure kind="stampede" number={1} />
<p>A run against a disposable PostgreSQL and Redis reproduced the arithmetic. With 400 requests a second for one expired key and the computation modelled as 150 ms, 61 source queries started before the first fill was stored at 151 ms.</p>
<p>Now let the source slow down. Sixty extra computations compete for the same processors and disks as everything else, and a 150 ms query can become a 600 ms one. The window then stays open four times as long, and 240 computations start: six times the fleet’s fill slots. That extra work slows the source further and holds the window open longer still, so a stampede on an expensive key feeds itself.</p>
<p>The same run with a 600 ms computation started 242 queries. This is what PostgreSQL showed about half a second in:</p>
<pre><code>{stampedeActivity}</code></pre>
<p>Hundreds of identical active statements, all about the same age, are the signature of a stampede. The wait event reads <code>PgSleep</code> because the run modelled card assembly with <code>pg_sleep</code>; in production it would be CPU, I/O or a lock.</p>
<p>The same estimate tells you when to do nothing. P7’s card arrives about 250 times a second and fills in 20 ms, so an expiry costs about five duplicate reads; the run gave six. A database absorbs that without anyone noticing. Estimate rate × fill time for your expensive answers before building machinery for all of them.</p>
<p>A daily hit ratio hides the burst entirely. With a 30-second TTL, the list is requested 12,000 times between expiries and misses 60 of them, a hit ratio of 99.5%. Those 60 misses still arrive together every 30 seconds, and the source must either be sized for them or be protected from them.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="share-work">
<h2 id="share-work">Share the work for one key</h2>
<p>The burst happens because callers cannot see each other’s work. Give each <code>Server</code> a map of loads in progress, keyed by the complete cache key. The first caller to miss registers a load and starts it. Callers that miss while it runs find the entry and wait for its result instead of starting their own.</p>
<p>When the load finishes, every waiter receives its result or its error, and the entry is removed. This is <strong>request coalescing</strong>, also called request collapsing, or singleflight after Go’s library. Within one process it turns 60 computations of the list into one.</p>
<p>Three details make it correct. Checking the map and registering a load must be one atomic step, or two callers can both decide they are first. The load checks the cache again before reading the source, because another process may have stored the answer since this caller’s lookup missed. And the map’s key identifies the same answer as the cache key: coalescing on <code>P7</code> alone would hand one shop’s card to another shop’s caller even if the cache keys were right, as <a href={hrefFor('cache-keys')}>Cache keys</a> explains.</p>
<p>A failed load passes its error to every waiter. None of them turns it into a cached “not found”, because a timeout says nothing about whether P7 exists. The entry is then removed. The next caller starts a fresh attempt, which passes through the same admission limit as any other source work.</p>
<p>Go provides this in <code>golang.org/x/sync/singleflight</code>. Java’s Caffeine does it inside <code>cache.get(key, loader)</code>, which runs the loader at most once per key, and in other languages a map of promises or futures does the same. All of them share work. None of them decides how long a caller may wait, how long the load may run or how much source work may start in total.</p>

<h3 id="bound-wait">Bound the wait as well as the work</h3>
<p>Every caller has its own deadline. A card request has 150 ms at the <code>Server</code>, and some of that has gone by the time it joins a load. Each caller must be able to leave when its own time runs out, without harming the work that other callers still need.</p>
<p>Suppose caller A misses P7’s card at 0 ms and starts a load that will take 100 ms, but A can wait only 50 ms. B joins at 20 ms and C at 40 ms, and each of them can wait 150 ms.</p>
<p>In most code the load runs under A’s request context, simply because A’s handler called it. A’s deadline then cancels the query at 50 ms, and B and C receive A’s timeout as their own error. Two callers who could have waited lose an answer that would have arrived at 100 ms.</p>
<p>So the load gets its own lifetime. It is detached from whichever caller started it and given its own budget, here 120 ms, so that a stalled source read still ends. Each caller then waits for the result or for its own deadline, whichever comes first. A caller whose deadline comes first returns what its contract allows, which is an older card the freshness rule still permits or an error, and starts nothing.</p>
<Code code={wrapper} lang="go" theme="css-variables" />
<p><code>context.WithoutCancel</code>, added in Go 1.21, keeps the first caller’s values but drops its cancellation and deadline, and <code>WithTimeout</code> then gives the load its 120 ms. <code>DoChan</code> returns a channel instead of blocking, so each caller can <code>select</code> on its own <code>ctx.Done()</code>. <code>Load</code> rechecks the cache, reads the source and fills; <code>Stale</code> returns a copy only if freshness still permits it.</p>
<p>In a run with A, B and C as above, the wrapper gave B and C the card at 102 ms from one source query. A received an error at 50 ms, or the older card when one was permitted. With the load tied to A’s context instead, all three callers failed at 50 ms.</p>
<MissFigure kind="sharedLoad" number={2} />
<p>The rule that matters most is in the last branch: a caller that gives up never starts its own read. nginx’s proxy cache shows what happens otherwise. With <code>proxy_cache_lock</code> on, one request fills a missing entry while the others wait. When <code>proxy_cache_lock_timeout</code> runs out, five seconds by default, a waiting request “will be passed to the proxied server, however, the response will not be cached”. Every waiter becomes uncached origin work at exactly the moment the origin is slow.</p>
<p>When the load’s own budget runs out, its waiters receive an error at 120 ms and the Go client cancels the query. In the run, pgx sent PostgreSQL a cancel request and the server logged <code>ERROR: canceling statement due to user request</code>.</p>
<p>Requesting cancellation is different from knowing that the work has stopped. A cancel request can be lost along with a broken connection. A client that simply disappears leaves its query running: in an earlier run on the same setup, a client killed one second into an eight-second query left the backend active until the query finished.</p>
<p>Two PostgreSQL settings bound the work where it runs. <code>statement_timeout</code> cancels any statement that runs past its limit. <code>client_connection_check_interval</code> makes a backend check for a vanished client while its query runs; set to 250 ms, it removed the killed client’s backend within a second. Both are off by default. Give the fill’s database role a statement timeout no longer than the load’s budget.</p>
<p>Bound the numbers as well as the times. <code>singleflight</code> keeps one channel per waiter and one entry per key, and limits neither. A slow source with many missing keys accumulates thousands of entries and waiters. Cap the waiters per key and the loads in flight per process, and give a caller that cannot join the same answer as one whose deadline has passed.</p>

<h3 id="instances">Across application instances</h3>
<p>The map lives in one process. Each of the 20 <code>Server</code> instances coalesces its own callers, so after one expiry each of them can run a load: up to 20 computations instead of one. In the same experiment, 20 separate groups, each taking every twentieth request, ran exactly 20 list queries.</p>
<p>Whether 20 is acceptable depends on the key. Each <code>Server</code> receives about 12 requests a second for P7’s card, so a 20 ms window rarely holds two of them. The fleet makes about five 20 ms reads per expiry, which is not worth coordinating. For the list, 20 computations of 150 ms occupy half the fleet’s 40 fill slots every 30 seconds, for one key. The list earns another mechanism and the card does not.</p>
<p>A <strong>lease</strong> gives one process permission to refill a key for a limited time. In Redis, <code>SET lease:&lt;key&gt; &lt;token&gt; NX PX 200</code> succeeds only if no lease exists, and the lease vanishes after 200 ms if its holder crashes. The token is a random value used for this attempt only. The <code>Server</code> that wins computes the list. A <code>Server</code> that loses serves the older list if freshness still permits it, or waits briefly and looks again within its caller’s deadline.</p>
<p>Facebook’s memcache issued lease tokens at most once every ten seconds per key, and on keys prone to herds its peak database load fell from 17,000 queries a second to 1,300.</p>
<p>A lease saves work; it does not make anything correct. Martin Kleppmann draws the distinction. An efficiency lock may occasionally let two holders through, and the cost is duplicate work. A correctness lock needs the protected resource itself to refuse a holder whose permission has expired. A refresh lease is the first kind. Whether the list it publishes is fresh enough is decided by the freshness rule and by conditional publication, never by holding the lease.</p>
<p>The weak point is a slow owner. Server A takes a 200 ms lease and starts the list query, which stalls for 900 ms. At 10 ms a request on Server B finds A’s lease and serves the older list.</p>
<p>At 200 ms A’s lease expires, although A is still working. At 210 ms B wins a new lease and computes the list in 150 ms. At 360 ms it publishes the list and releases its lease. Between 210 and 360 ms the list is computed twice at once. At 900 ms A’s query returns.</p>
<MissFigure kind="lease" number={3} />
<p>What A does next decides whether the slow owner only wasted work or also damaged the cache. A read its list from a snapshot taken at 0 ms, older than B’s. A plain <code>SET</code> would replace B’s newer list with it. A plain <code>DEL</code> of the lease would remove whichever lease exists by then, perhaps a third <code>Server</code>’s. Both operations must check A’s token atomically. On Redis 7.0.15 the run printed:</p>
<pre><code>{leaseTrace}</code></pre>
<p>With a plain <code>SET</code> in place of A’s checked publish, the same run ended with A’s older list in the cache. Redis 7 needs a short Lua script for each check, because Redis runs a script without interleaving other commands:</p>
<pre><code>{ownerChecks}</code></pre>
<p>From Redis 8.4, <code>DELEX key IFEQ token</code> releases a matching lease in one command. Publication here still needs the script, because the condition is on the lease key and the write is to the list key. <code>SET … IFEQ</code> compares a key with its own current value, which suits the design in <a href={hrefFor('versioned-fills')}>Versioned cache fills</a>, where the reservation and the answer share one key.</p>
<p>The lease lifetime bounded A’s permission, not A’s work. PostgreSQL never heard of the lease, and A’s query ran for 900 ms whatever Redis recorded. Bound the work where it runs.</p>
<p>With <code>statement_timeout</code> set to 180 ms for A’s fill, shorter than the 200 ms lease, PostgreSQL cancelled A’s query at 181 ms with <code>ERROR: canceling statement due to statement timeout</code>. A released its own lease, and B computed the list alone. Order the limits so that the statement timeout is shorter than the load’s budget, and the budget shorter than the lease. A process can still pause between its query and its publish, for garbage collection or a lost scheduling slot, so publication keeps its token check anyway.</p>
<p>If the lease store itself is unavailable, the <code>Server</code> instances fall back to per-process coalescing, 20 computations, through the same source admission as everything else. Treating “no lease service” as “everyone may load” would remove the protection when the cache tier is already in trouble.</p>
<p>memcached offers the same idea inside the cache server. On a miss, its meta protocol’s <code>N</code> flag creates a placeholder in the same round trip as the lookup. The first client receives <code>W</code>, meaning it should fill the entry; later clients receive <code>Z</code>, meaning someone else is filling it.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="early-refresh">
<h2 id="early-refresh">Refresh while an answer is still usable</h2>
<p>Everything so far happens after the entry has expired, when callers have nothing to serve. A hot entry can be replaced before that point. One request starts a refresh while the others keep receiving the current answer, which is still within its allowance.</p>
<p>Two times govern this, and they must stay separate. The refresh point is when the application starts trying to replace the answer. The last allowed serving time comes from the freshness rule: for the list, 30 seconds after its observation. A successful refresh replaces the answer with a new observation. A failed refresh leaves the old answer exactly as old as it was, to be served until its last allowed time and not after. Refreshing early spends part of the allowance on replacement work; it never extends the allowance.</p>
<MissFigure kind="refresh" number={4} />
<p>Practitioners call this pair a soft TTL and a hard TTL. HTTP names the same idea <code>stale-while-revalidate</code>, and Caffeine’s <code>refreshAfterWrite</code> returns the old value while one refresh runs, keeping it if the refresh fails. <a href={hrefFor('freshness')}>Freshness and invalidation</a> decides how long an answer may be served and whether a failure permits serving it longer; early refresh works inside whatever it decides.</p>
<p>A fixed refresh point on a hot key brings the stampede back in a politer form. If every request after 25 seconds may start a refresh, then every request that arrives before the first refresh lands will start one. For the list that is 60 again, although callers now receive the old list instead of waiting. A fixed point therefore needs coalescing, and for the list a lease, to keep it to one refresh.</p>
<p><strong>Probabilistic early expiration</strong>, published as XFetch by Vattani, Chierichetti and Lowenstein, spreads the decision across the requests themselves. Store the computation time Δ beside the value. Each request then refreshes early if</p>
<p class="miss-formula"><code>now − Δ · β · ln(rand()) ≥ expiry</code></p>
<p>where <code>rand()</code> is uniform between 0 and 1, and β is a tuning factor, 1 by default. Because −ln(rand()) is usually small and occasionally large, most requests long before expiry do nothing, and the chance of refreshing rises steeply as expiry approaches. The paper shows that this exponential spread is the best possible shape for the purpose.</p>
<p>The first refresh starts roughly Δ · β · ln(rate · Δ · β) before expiry. For the list, at 400 requests a second with Δ = 150 ms, that is 0.15 × ln 60, about 0.6 s. In 2,000 simulated expiries it started 0.7 s early on average.</p>
<p>Requests that arrive while the first refresh runs may also trigger one. An expiry produced 2.8 refreshes on average across the fleet; the median was 2 and the 99th percentile 11. With β = 2, refreshes started about 1.6 s early and averaged 1.6 per expiry. Pair XFetch with per-process coalescing, and add the lease if even 11 is too many.</p>
<p>XFetch needs no coordination and does no work for keys nobody reads, because an unread key simply expires. A timer does the opposite. Refreshing all 50,000 cards every 25 seconds would cost 2,000 fills a second, the entire allowance, mostly for cards that are rarely requested. A fixed refresh point with jitter is enough when rate × fill time is small, as for P7’s card at about five. XFetch earns its place on keys like the list, where that product is large.</p>

<h3 id="jitter">Spread refreshes across keys</h3>
<p>Jitter solves a different problem: keys written together expire together. Suppose a warm-up fills 10,000 cards in a few seconds, or a deploy changes a key prefix. Those entries all reach the end of their 30 seconds at about the same moment, and the source sees the warm-up again one TTL later. Spread them by choosing each key’s refresh point at random inside the allowance, say between 22 and 26 seconds, or by subtracting a random amount from the TTL. Never add the jitter beyond the allowance.</p>
<p>Neither jitter nor early refresh helps in two cases. Jitter separates keys; it does nothing for the 400 callers of one key. And when Redis restarts empty there is no entry left to refresh and no expiry to spread, because every key is missing at once. That needs a limit on all the work sent to the source.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="source-budget">
<h2 id="source-budget">Limit source work across all keys</h2>
<p>Coalescing combines callers of the same key, but after a cold restart most misses are for different cards. A simulation of the shop’s popularity model shows how little there is to combine. With 50,000 cards, Zipf-like popularity, 20,000 requests a second over 20 <code>Server</code> instances and 20 ms fills, per-process coalescing absorbs about 12% of the misses. Perfect fleet-wide coalescing would absorb about 36%. The rest are distinct source reads, and something must cap how many of them start.</p>
<p>The cap that protects the source is concurrency: how many fills may run at once. Little’s law connects it to throughput, because the average number of fills in progress equals the rate at which they start times the time each one takes. The source allowance is 2,000 card fills a second at a median of 20 ms, so the fleet may have 2,000 × 0.02 = 40 fills in flight. That is 2 executing slots on each <code>Server</code>.</p>
<p>A <code>Server</code> whose two slots are busy holds at most one more request in a waiting place; Google’s SRE book recommends queues of half the worker count or less. When the waiting request reaches a slot, check its remaining deadline again. If that no longer covers a fill, about 60 ms at the 99th percentile, refuse it instead of starting it. A request that finds the slots and the waiting place full is refused at once. Deciding whether to start work at all is <strong>admission control</strong>.</p>
<MissFigure kind="fleet" number={5} />
<p>A concurrency limit keeps protecting the source when the source slows, at the cost of throughput. If fills slow from 20 ms to 40 ms, the 40 slots complete only 1,000 a second; at 200 ms, 200 a second. That is the right behaviour: the slower the source, the less work it receives.</p>
<p>A rate limit of 2,000 starts a second does the opposite and keeps admitting 2,000 a second as each one takes longer, which at 200 ms is 400 fills in flight. Netflix’s concurrency-limits library adjusts the limit from measured latency for this reason, since a fixed rate goes out of date as soon as the source’s speed changes. Rate limits remain useful for work you want spread evenly, such as a warm-up.</p>
<p>Everything that reads the source counts against the same allowance: demand fills, refreshes, warm-up reads and retries. A refresher that bypasses admission can take the last slots from callers who are waiting for answers.</p>
<p>Refuse quickly. A rejection that takes a millisecond costs almost nothing, while a queue that holds a request for 140 ms and then times out has spent the caller’s whole budget for no answer. Count refusals separately from successful replies, and keep their fast latency out of the success latency. Otherwise the dashboard improves as fewer customers receive cards. What a refused caller receives is the subject of the next section.</p>
<p>Per-<code>Server</code> limits add up, so check the sum whenever the fleet changes. Scaled to 30 instances with 2 slots each, the fleet admits 60 fills in flight. That is 3,000 a second at 20 ms, past the 2,500 at which checkout failed in the load test.</p>
<p>Recompute the share from the fleet size. Forty slots over 30 instances is 1⅓ each, so give each instance 1 slot, or a rate of about 67 fills a second. Alternatively, share one limiter across the fleet or enforce the limit at the source. Autoscaling that adds instances under load is where an unadjusted limit matters most.</p>

<h3 id="pools">A connection pool is not admission control</h3>
<p>Suppose a teammate proposes protecting the database with a 50-connection pool on each <code>Server</code> and no other limit. Across 20 instances that permits 1,000 fills in flight, 25 times what the source can carry and ten times PostgreSQL’s default <code>max_connections</code> of 100. The 101st connection is refused with <code>FATAL: sorry, too many clients already</code>.</p>
<p>While fills take 20 ms the pools sit mostly idle and the problem stays hidden. When fills slow to 200 ms, the normal 1,200 misses a second need 240 fills in flight, the pools let them all through, and the database slows further. Callers then queue inside the pool for a connection, with no deadline check, and time out after their budget has gone.</p>
<p>A pool does not remove duplicates either. In an earlier stampede run, shrinking the pool from 50 connections to 10 cut peak active queries from 49 to 9. It left 107 of the 110 queries in place, and the 99th-percentile caller latency rose from 107 ms to 507 ms. The pool converted concurrency into queueing.</p>
<p>Specify the limits instead. Each <code>Server</code> gets 2 executing fills and 1 waiting place, derived from the fleet allowance, with a deadline check when a request leaves the waiting place. The pool is large enough for the admitted fills plus the <code>Server</code>’s other queries, and the fill role carries a <code>statement_timeout</code>. At 30 instances the per-instance numbers change as above.</p>

<h3 id="batching">Batch distinct misses</h3>
<p>Coalescing cannot combine misses for different keys, but one request sometimes carries many of them. The list page renders 24 product cards. If eight are missing, eight separate fills cost eight round trips and eight slots, while one query with <code>WHERE id = ANY($1)</code> fetches all eight together. This is the DataLoader pattern. Collect the keys missed during one request, or during a tick of a millisecond or two. Load them in one query, then hand each caller its own row.</p>
<p>Count a batch against the allowance by its cost, since a batch of eight holds its slot longer than one card does, and keep batches small enough to finish within the callers’ deadlines. Batching saves round trips and per-query overhead. The source still reads every row.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="cache-unavailable">
<h2 id="cache-unavailable">When the cache is unavailable</h2>
<p>Code that treats every failed lookup as a miss confuses two different events. A miss is an answer from the cache: the key is not there. A timeout says nothing about the key, and it has already spent time. With a 10 ms command timeout inside a 150 ms budget, a request that timed out on Redis has at most 140 ms left for everything else. Do not give the source a fresh 150 ms.</p>
<p>Then count the work that arrives. With a hit ratio h, the source sees the fraction 1 − h of requests. Lose the cache and it sees all of them, a multiplication of 1 / (1 − h). At the shop’s 94% that is about 17: the 1,200 fills a second of normal service become 20,000 attempts. Per-process coalescing removes about 12% of them, which leaves roughly 17,700 a second, nearly nine times the allowance.</p>
<p>A better cache makes this worse, since at 99% the multiplication is 100. Bronson and colleagues use this example to show how a cache hides the source’s real capacity: the application handles ten times the database’s load only while the cache is warm.</p>
<p>With admission in place, the source still receives its 2,000 fills a second, and each fill answers its caller plus anyone who joined it. In the simulation, about 12% of requests received a full card. The other 88% need an answer the application has defined in advance:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A local copy the freshness rule still permits.</strong> If each <code>Server</code> keeps its own copies of the cards it served most recently, the popular ones are there, and the 1,000 most requested cards carry two thirds of all requests. Keeping those 1,000 cards within the 30-second allowance on all 20 instances costs 20 × 1,000 ÷ 30, about 670 fills a second: a third of the allowance, for as long as the outage lasts.</li>
<li><strong>A reduced card</strong>: the name and picture, with the price marked as temporarily unavailable. Never GBP 0.00 and never “product not found”, because those are different answers. Checkout still reads the authoritative price, so a reduced card cannot sell anything at a wrong price.</li>
<li><strong>A fast refusal</strong>, such as HTTP 503, that callers are told not to retry.</li>
</ul>
<p>Facebook’s memcache took another route when individual cache servers failed. Rehashing a dead server’s keys onto the survivors would move its hot keys with them, and “a single key can account for 20% of a server’s requests”. Clients instead send the failed server’s keys to a gutter pool, about 1% of the cluster’s servers, holding entries with short TTLs.</p>
<p>Bound the cache calls themselves, with command timeouts and a cap on pending cache requests, so a stalled cache cannot hold every <code>Server</code> waiting.</p>
<p>After repeated failures a <code>Server</code> may stop calling the cache for a while and send a few probe lookups to detect its recovery. Skipping the cache saves the 10 ms timeout on every request; every request that then goes to the source still passes through admission. A switch like this is a circuit breaker, and AWS warns that its two modes make behaviour hard to test, so test both. <a href={hrefFor('redis')}>Caching with Redis</a> sets the client timeouts and the optional-fill branch; the requirement here holds for any client.</p>

<h3 id="retries">Retries multiply the work</h3>
<p>Suppose the card loader retries a failed source read up to three times, its database client does the same, and so does the edge proxy in front of the <code>Server</code> instances. During a source brownout every layer sees failures, and each attempt at one layer becomes up to three at the next. One request for one card can then cause 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 source queries. AWS’s version of the example, with five layers, reaches 3⁵ = 243 times the database load.</p>
<p>Retry at one layer: the loader, which knows the request’s remaining deadline and sends every attempt through coalescing and admission. Configure the database client and the proxy not to retry these reads. Cap attempts per request at three, and only while the deadline still allows a useful attempt.</p>
<p>Budget retries across requests as well, because a per-request cap still triples the load when everything fails. Google’s SRE book retries only while retries are under 10% of a client’s requests. The AWS SDKs draw retries from a token bucket of 500 tokens, 5 per retry, refilled by successes. Mark admission refusals and overload errors as not retryable. Backoff and jitter spread retries out, but they create no capacity.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="cold-recovery">
<h2 id="cold-recovery">Return from a cold cache</h2>
<p>Redis restarts empty at peak. It answers <code>PING</code> at once and holds no cards. Every request misses, admission holds fills at 2,000 a second, and in the first moments nine requests in ten receive the defined fallback.</p>
<p>Recovery is quick because popularity is skewed. Misses arrive in proportion to popularity, so the most requested cards are among the first filled, and each fill turns every later request for that card into a hit. In the simulation, 51% of requests in the second quarter-second were hits, 61% after one second, 70% after two and about 80% after five.</p>
<p>The 1,000 most requested cards serve two thirds of all requests. Even if every fill went to them, they could not all be back in under half a second, since 1,000 fills at 2,000 a second take 0.5 s. Under demand fills, the top 500 were back within 0.8 s and 900 of them within 2.6 s. The last few stragglers took about 8 seconds, by which time they hardly mattered.</p>
<MissFigure kind="recovery" number={6} />
<p>Prewarming all 50,000 cards first would slow this down. 50,000 fills at 2,000 a second occupy the whole allowance for 25 seconds. In catalogue order they restore popularity evenly, one twenty-fifth each second: 20% of requests after five seconds, against 80% from demand fills. Meanwhile demand misses for the hottest cards wait behind the warm-up, and memory fills with cards few people open.</p>
<p>A bounded warm-up helps when it is targeted. Loading yesterday’s 2,000 most requested cards takes one second of the allowance and restores about 70% of hits. Give a warm-up a share of the allowance, not all of it, and let demand misses come first.</p>
<p>Bring traffic back the same way. If requests were held back upstream or new instances are starting, increase admitted traffic in steps and watch two measurements at each step. Source fill latency should stay near its normal 20 ms median and 60 ms 99th percentile. Admitted source work should stay below the 40 fills in flight, with refusals falling rather than rising. Increase while both hold; stop when latency climbs or admission starts refusing.</p>
<p>Slack’s February 2022 incident shows the cost of a step too large. After throttling client boot to protect the database, a first increase overloaded it again, and the team had to cut back and raise the limit “by smaller increments”. <a href={hrefFor('operating-caches')}>Operating a cache</a> owns the rollout procedure; the point here is that recovery spends the same allowance as everything else.</p>

<h3 id="metastable">When the cache cannot refill itself</h3>
<p>Ten minutes after a cache failover, the dashboard shows a hit ratio flat at 31% and database CPU at 100%. <code>pg_stat_activity</code> lists 90 identical card queries, the oldest running for 4.8 s, against an application timeout of 1 s. Almost no fills succeed, and the loader records 2.7 source attempts per request.</p>
<p>This is a <strong>metastable failure</strong>. The trigger, the failover’s cold cache, has passed, but a sustaining effect keeps the system failed, and each part feeds the next.</p>
<p>The low hit ratio sends too much work to the database. The overloaded database takes longer than 1 s, so the application gives up, the fill never stores its answer, and the hit ratio stays low. Meanwhile the queries run on after the application has left, the oldest for almost five times the timeout, and each abandoned query is replaced by a retry.</p>
<p>At 2.7 attempts per request, the database does nearly three times the work of the requests it is failing. Load falling back to normal would not cure it. The HotOS paper that named metastable failures uses exactly this look-aside cache as its example. A later study of real incidents found retries sustaining more than half of them, with outages commonly lasting four to ten hours. Two changes break the loop:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop the amplification.</strong> Turn off retries outside the loader and budget them inside it, and put the admission limit in front of the database: 40 fills in flight, the rest refused quickly. Admitted fills then finish in their normal 20 ms, store their answers, and the hit ratio starts to rise.</li>
<li><strong>Stop abandoned work at the source.</strong> Give the fill role a <code>statement_timeout</code> no longer than the application’s remaining budget, so the queries nobody is waiting for are cancelled instead of running for seconds.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then raise traffic in steps as above. Without an admission limit, the emergency equivalent is shedding load at the edge until the database recovers. Facebook faced a harsher version in 2010. A configuration check treated database errors as invalid values and deleted the cached keys, which sent ever more queries to the failing databases. The site was turned off to break the loop.</p>

<h3 id="exercise">Exercise the whole design</h3>
<p>Each mechanism has a failure it exists for, so test each failure on purpose, with the cache disabled as well as empty:</p>
<div class="cache-table-scroll"><table>
<caption>What each rehearsal should show</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Change</th><th scope="col">Expected observation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Slow the list’s computation</th><td>One load per key per <code>Server</code>, or one per fleet with the lease. Callers leave at their deadlines; no caller starts another read; the statement timeout ends the query.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Fail an early refresh</th><td>The older answer is served until its last allowed time, and not after.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Miss on many distinct keys</th><td>Fills in flight stay at or below the fleet limit; refused callers receive the defined fallback within a few milliseconds.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Make Redis unreachable</th><td>Source work stays inside the allowance; each request’s remaining deadline is respected; the lease falls back to per-process coalescing.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Restart Redis empty</th><td>Hits return in popularity order; warm-up and retries spend the same allowance.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Count useful replies, refusals, fallbacks and actual source work separately. Fast refusals lower the average latency while fewer customers receive cards, and client timeouts lower the request count while abandoned queries still run. Only separate counters tell these outcomes apart.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="numbers">
<h2 id="numbers">Where the numbers come from</h2>
<p>The shop’s production scale is an invented example set at realistic magnitudes. Its traffic, fleet, hit ratio, fill times, load test and list are teaching facts; they describe no running service.</p>
<p>Counts and outputs described as runs were observed on 6 November 2026 on one Linux host. It ran a disposable PostgreSQL 16.15 and Redis 7.0.15, and Go 1.24.7 with <code>golang.org/x/sync</code> v0.10.0, <code>go-redis</code> v9.7.0 and <code>pgx</code> v5.7.1. The source query added <code>pg_sleep</code> to model card assembly and the list computation, so the durations were chosen and the counts, states, errors and Redis replies were observed.</p>
<p>The wrapper above is the code that was run. The pool, killed-client and <code>too many clients</code> results come from the planning runs for this article on the same setup.</p>
<p>The XFetch figures come from 2,000 simulated expiries with Poisson arrivals. The cold-restart and outage shares come from a simulation of the example. It used 50,000 cards with Zipf-like popularity of exponent 1, the fleet’s slots and waiting places, 20 ms fills and the 150 ms budget. It modelled no expiry during the first 30 seconds. They show orders of magnitude and orderings, not predictions for another workload. The Facebook, Slack, nginx, AWS and Google figures are from the sources below.</p>
</section>

